<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleusis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elefsina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleusis is an ancient town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, about 18 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, now called Elefsina. It was home to the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a> where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusinian-mysteries">Eleusinian Mysteries</a>, the most famous secret religious rites of the Greek world, were celebrated for many centuries. The Costoboci sacked the sanctuary around 170 CE, and the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> paid for its restoration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleusis.jpg" alt="Eleusis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_view_of_sanctuary_of_Demeter_and_Kore_and_the_Telesterion_(Initiation_Hall),_center_for_the_Eleusinian_Mysteries,_Eleusis_(8191841684).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
